<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Celestino da Silva</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1849">1849</a>–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Celestino da Silva was a Portuguese army officer who governed Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a> from 1894 to 1908. Through repeated military campaigns against the island's kingdoms he imposed colonial control over much of the interior, and he promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantations, partly through companies of his own. Resentment of the colonial order he built culminated in the Manufahi revolt of 1912.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-celestino-da-silva.jpg" alt="José Celestino da Silva" width="294" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_Celestino_da_Silva.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Celestino da Silva was born in Vilar de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquae-flaviae">Chaves</a>, Portugal, on 6 January 1849. He graduated from the School of the Army in 1865 as best in his class and became an ensign in 1869. In that rank he was attached to Prince Carlos, later Carlos I of Portugal, and the two became friends. He was promoted lieutenant in 1875 and captain in the 2nd Lancers Regiment in 1883.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Appointed major and governor of Portuguese Timor in 1894, he launched three major offensives in 1894-95 against certain kingdoms, drew up terms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> with several liurai, or native petty kings, and put down various rebellions, in more than 20 military actions in all. He replaced the finta tax, paid in kind, with a poll tax and founded schools that taught the basics of agriculture for export <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> growing. He set up regular sea links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> network several hundred kilometres long, had the swamps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dili">Dili</a> drained, gave the town a water supply and in 1906 built a modern hospital.</p><p>He also used his office to enrich himself at the expense of the Portuguese state and of the Timorese. He was involved in or owned almost all the private plantation companies founded under his rule and illegally supplied them with Timorese forced labourers. In 1897 he set up the Sociedade Agrícola Pátria e Trabalho, which gained wide monopolies in the colony. Contemporary critics mockingly called him King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Only after the assassination of his friend King Carlos I in 1908 was he recalled. His wife died on the voyage home. In Portugal he commanded a cavalry regiment in Almeida, and in 1910, after the proclamation of the Republic, he was promoted general and placed in the reserve. He died on 10 February 1911. In 1932 he was posthumously made a Grand Officer of the Order of the Colonial Empire, and his portrait appeared on Portuguese Timorese escudo banknotes from January 1960.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José Celestino da Silva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governor <b>José Celestino da Silva</b> extended control by force, and the Manufahi revolt of 1912 was crushed.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-timor">Portuguese Timor</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vilar de Nantes, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.71,-7.45&amp;year=1889" aria-label="Vilar de Nantes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of José Celestino da Silva"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4526 3990h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4526 3990h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.71,-7.45&amp;year=1889">What was here in 1889? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
